# Sway config -- stow-managed from ~/Desktop/configs/sway/config.
# Edit it here, not at ~/.config/sway/config (that is a symlink into this repo).
# Apply changes with `swaymsg reload` or $mod+Ctrl+c.
#
# Binding grammar (adopted 2026-08-22, DECISIONS.md item 6; widened 2026-09-28
# for the tree tier and the monitor axis, and 2026-09-29 for the shaping verbs
# on Shift and the third clause). The modifier names what the action
# acts on -- to place or recall a binding, ask what it acts on:
#
#   $mod          you: your attention and your instruments. Move focus
#                 (hjkl/arrows, space between the tiling and floating
#                 layers), climb and descend the tree (a parent, z child),
#                 visit workspaces (1-0, Tab), summon things (launchers,
#                 menu, scratchpad show), clear a notification (n), toggle
#                 your equipment (w bar, m the pointing devices).
#   $mod+Shift    the focused window: move it (hjkl/arrows), send it (1-0),
#                 stash it (minus), toggle its state (f fullscreen,
#                 space floating), split it (v: the next opens below,
#                 b: beside) and fold it with its siblings (s: tabs).
#   $mod+Ctrl     the enclosures: the container around the window (r rotates
#                 it, a row into a column and back) and the session around
#                 everything (l lock, c reload).
#   $mod+$alt     the workspaces, as the monitors stand: walk this monitor's
#                 row (left/right), step to the monitor above or below
#                 (up/down); +Shift carries the focused window along, so
#                 Shift keeps its meaning composed.
#
# Three clauses complete the law:
#
#   * Key families override the table on their own key. Escape means LEAVING,
#     graded by weight: $mod+Escape ends the session, +Shift reaches the
#     machine (poweroff/reboot nag), and with a window holding the keyboard
#     it leaves that capture instead. Print means capture; XF86* means
#     hardware (the power key opens the machine nag). The mouse is its own
#     family: $mod with a drag handles the window under the pointer, moving
#     it (left) or resizing it (right), tiled or floating, and a middle
#     click on a fold's tab title closes that window. The clause is forced
#     by Print regardless (Shift+Print is a capture variant, no window
#     involved), so Escape's gradient rides it for free.
#
#   * Promotions are a stated, closed list: q kill and r resize are window
#     verbs sitting one tier light because they are pressed all day. The
#     default home for a new window verb is Shift; promotion is earned by
#     daily frequency and recorded here.
#
#   * A second launcher on a letter takes Shift: Return is Ghostty and
#     Shift+Return WezTerm, x adelotype and Shift+x the plain editor, d rofi
#     and Shift+d wofi. Shift there means the other one; the window tier
#     lends the chord (2026-09-29).
#
# Placing a new binding: an app -> $mod+mnemonic letter, and a second app on
# the same letter -> its Shift. A window verb ->
# $mod+Shift+letter. An op on the container around the window, or on the
# session -> $mod+Ctrl+letter. A machine action -> a button on the
# $mod+Shift+Escape nag before it ever claims a chord. Hardware with no XF86
# key -> a bare-tier toggle letter (m, the pointing devices, is the precedent). A split
# or a fold shapes the focused window's place, so it is a window verb ->
# $mod+Shift+letter (v, b, s); the shape of the container around it stays
# Ctrl's (r). The Layout section has the case.
#
# Both vim keys and arrow keys are bound for every motion, on purpose -- the
# parity is maintained deliberately, so add arrow variants to any new motion.
#
# Caution when editing: `sway --validate` does NOT check the command body of a
# bindsym. A typo there passes validation and fails silently at runtime. Verify
# bindings by running the command directly: `swaymsg -- <the command>` -- with
# one reservation. Sway rewrites a body in two passes before sh sees it.
# Loading this file un-escapes backslash escapes (\" \\ \n \t), which swaymsg
# does not, so a body with a backslash behaves differently pasted to swaymsg
# than fired from a key (\@ is left alone, and sh drops the backslash). And
# every $name that begins with a defined variable is replaced, at load and
# again at each press, swaymsg included, so '$$name' is no escape; '${name}'
# survives both passes. The desktop suite runs every non-exec body against a
# nested sway loaded from this file, which is the check that sees the file's
# own pass (measured 2026-09-30).
#
# Read `man 5 sway` for a complete reference.


### Variables

set $mod Mod4
set $alt Mod1

# Home row direction keys, like vim.
set $left h
set $down j
set $up k
set $right l

# Two terminals, deliberately: $term is Ghostty, the daily one since the
# comparison of 2026-09-03 closed on 2026-09-05
# (docs/ghostty-vs-wezterm-2026-09-03.md), and $term2 WezTerm, one keystroke
# away for what Ghostty cannot do. Since 2026-09-28 sway runs on bigfed too,
# so the choice binds both machines.
# --gtk-single-instance=false so each press spawns its own process and so
# lands in working-directory; new windows/tabs/splits raised from inside one
# still inherit. Reasoning in ghostty/config's Window section.
set $term ghostty --gtk-single-instance=false
set $term2 wezterm
# The app menu: rofi since 2026-09-30 (its package, rofi/, holds the why),
# with wofi behind it on Shift as the second launcher.
set $menu  rofi -show drun
set $menu2 wofi --show drun


### Output configuration

# `~` is expanded here (sway runs background paths through wordexp), and an
# unreadable path is a hard config error rather than a silent fallback, so a
# typo in this path fails loudly at load.
output * bg ~/Desktop/configs/wallpapers/monarch1080dark.png fill

# bigfed's two monitors, matched by make, model and serial (as `swaymsg -t
# get_outputs` reports them), so these lines apply only where those monitors
# are plugged in: a line naming an absent monitor is ignored, at start and at
# reload (tested 2026-09-28 and 2026-09-30), which leaves fedxps untouched.
# Port names are not stable here -- the Samsung was HDMI-A-2 in the 2026-08-05
# firmware inventory and is DP-3 now. The arrangement and modes are GNOME's
# (~/.config/monitors.xml): the MSI above the Samsung, 862 px in from its
# left edge, both at 60 Hz. 60 Hz is deliberate -- cooler, and nothing here
# needs more; left to itself sway picks 120 and 100 Hz. The GPU's memory
# clock sits at its top level, 35 W, at either rate under sway (measured
# 2026-09-28). Adaptive sync is off since 2026-09-30: bigfed's record keeps
# VRR off after two amdgpu hangs (2026-08-06) pending a controlled re-trial,
# which the 2026-09-28 lines had contradicted; `adaptive_sync on` returns to
# both lines when that trial passes. Any other output is auto-enabled and
# placed in a row: one plugged in later goes to the right of those already
# on, while screens present when sway starts are placed newest first, so on
# fedxps a display attached at login lands left of the panel and takes
# workspace 1 (measured on a nested sway, 2026-09-30).
set $samsung "Samsung Electric Company C49RG9x H1AK500000"
set $msi     "Microstep MSI MAG341CQ MA0H050010368"
output $msi     position 862 0  mode 3440x1440@60.016Hz
output $samsung position 0 1440 mode 5120x1440@59.977Hz

# fedxps's own panel, the one output named by its connector: eDP-1 is the
# laptop's built-in port, and bigfed has none, so no other screen matches it.
# With max_render_time off, sway composites the moment a frame has been
# shown, and on i915 that commit can reach the kernel before the previous
# commit's record of the switched-off cursor plane is closed. The pointer is
# off whenever Ghostty hides it for typing, a video hides it, or a screen
# share draws it into the stream. The kernel then refuses the commit (EBUSY),
# the journal logs "Page-flip failed on output eDP-1", and the screen keeps
# the old frame until the next redraw (i915 kernel issue 13410; wlroots
# issue 3507 and MR 5454, open as of 2026-09-30). Compositing 10 ms before
# the refresh leaves the kernel some 6 ms to close that record, and lets a
# client's new frame reach the screen up to one refresh sooner; a frame that
# takes sway longer than 10 ms to composite shows one refresh late. On trial
# from 2026-09-30: the journal's count of that error per day says whether
# it works.
output eDP-1 max_render_time 10

# Workspaces 1-5 live on the Samsung and 6-10 on the MSI, so on bigfed a
# workspace number also names a monitor: $mod+Shift+6 sends a window up to the
# MSI. A pin naming an absent monitor is ignored, so on fedxps every workspace
# opens on the laptop's screen as before (tested 2026-09-28).
workspace 1  output $samsung
workspace 2  output $samsung
workspace 3  output $samsung
workspace 4  output $samsung
workspace 5  output $samsung
workspace 6  output $msi
workspace 7  output $msi
workspace 8  output $msi
workspace 9  output $msi
workspace 10 output $msi


### Input configuration

# The touchpad, set as GNOME sets it on the same laptop
# (org.gnome.desktop.peripherals.touchpad, read 2026-09-30), so a finger
# means the same thing under both. The block began as upstream's example and
# is live; it parts from that example where GNOME does:
#   click_method clickfinger -- GNOME's 'fingers': a press with two fingers
#     anywhere is a right click, three a middle one. Left unset, libinput
#     uses button areas, the bottom-right corner for right. middle_emulation
#     went with the areas: under clickfinger it does nothing, and GNOME has
#     it off.
#   drag_lock disabled -- a tap-drag ends when the finger lifts, as under
#     GNOME. sway 1.11 alone forces enabled_sticky, where the button stays
#     held until another tap (swaywm/sway#8758, reverted upstream in 1.12);
#     stated, it holds whatever the release.
# Comments sit on lines of their own throughout this file: sway takes # as a
# comment only at the start of a line and hands a trailing one to the command
# as more arguments (2026-09-30). dwt is disable-while-typing; tap is tap to
# click. Devices can also be addressed one by one -- see `man 5 sway-input`.
input type:touchpad {
    dwt enabled
    tap enabled
    natural_scroll enabled
    click_method clickfinger
    drag_lock disabled
}

# Key repeat as GNOME has it: delay 500 ms, and repeat-interval 30 ms, which
# mutter hands clients as 1000/30 = 33 keys a second. sway's own defaults are
# 600 ms and 25. Held bindings repeat at this pace too (man 5 sway,
# --no-repeat, and the rule under Key bindings). The values must stay
# digits: sway reads a word here as 0, which turns repeat off.
input type:keyboard {
    repeat_delay 500
    repeat_rate 33
}


### Key bindings

# Held keys repeat: once a key has been held for the repeat delay sway runs
# its binding again at the repeat rate (500 ms, then 33 a second: the
# keyboard block above) unless the binding carries --no-repeat (man 5 sway,
# bindsym). Motions, the walks, the carries along a row, volume, brightness
# and the resize keys repeat on purpose; every toggle, every launcher and
# every verb that would go on to the next window -- kill, send, stash --
# carries --no-repeat, so a long press does one thing (2026-09-30). A new
# binding follows the same split.

#
# Session, power, and the bar
#
    # Reload re-reads this config: a session op, so Ctrl tier, c = config. It
    # sat on $mod+Shift+c (upstream's default) until 2026-08-22; only the
    # modifier moved, so that Shift could mean the focused window and nothing
    # else. A reload does not refuse a file with a bad line: sway skips that
    # line, applies the rest, and raises its own red nag, whose first button,
    # "Exit sway", ends the session at one click; only an unreadable file is
    # refused (measured on a nested sway, 2026-09-30). Validate first.
    bindsym $mod+Ctrl+c reload

    # swaynag gotcha: only -z/-Z buttons dismiss the nag; -b/-B run their action
    # and leave the bar on screen. The dismiss button is built in -- `-s` only
    # renames it, which is how "Cancel" below is made to actually cancel.
    # Use -B rather than -b: -b routes the action through $TERMINAL when that
    # variable is set, which is not wanted for either of these.
    # swaynag reads no keys, only the pointer, and it has no single-instance
    # guard. Either Escape chord closes whatever nag is open -- its own, the
    # other's, sway's config-error nag -- and the next press opens its own:
    # pkill's status decides, as on $mod+d (2026-09-30).
    bindsym --no-repeat $mod+Escape exec pkill -x swaynag || swaynag -t warning -s 'Cancel' \
        -m 'You pressed the exit shortcut. Do you really want to exit sway? This will end your Wayland session.' \
        -B 'Yes, exit sway' 'swaymsg exit'

    # The same chord while a window holds a keyboard-shortcuts inhibitor (a VM
    # viewer, a remote desktop, a page that has locked the keyboard): sway
    # grants inhibitors unasked (seat shortcuts_inhibitor defaults to
    # enable), and then every chord in this file goes to that window. This
    # twin is exempt and takes the keys back, as Super+Escape does under
    # GNOME; it is live only while an inhibitor is (man 5 sway, --inhibited;
    # 2026-09-30).
    bindsym --inhibited --no-repeat $mod+Escape shortcuts_inhibitor disable

    # Machine actions share this one nag rather than each claiming a chord
    # (Escape family: leaving, graded -- the session on $mod+Escape, the
    # machine one key heavier). Reboot joined as a second button 2026-08-22,
    # settling a parked $mod+Shift+r idea that predated the nag.
    bindsym --no-repeat $mod+Shift+Escape exec pkill -x swaynag || swaynag -t warning -s 'Cancel' \
        -m 'Shut down or reboot?' \
        -B 'Poweroff' 'systemctl poweroff' \
        -B 'Reboot' 'systemctl reboot'

    # The power key opens the same nag, now that logind leaves the key to
    # sway (the inhibitor under Includes and startup). Until 2026-09-30 one
    # press powered the machine off with no question, on both machines. Not
    # --locked: nothing draws over the lock screen.
    bindsym --no-repeat XF86PowerOff exec pkill -x swaynag || swaynag -t warning -s 'Cancel' \
        -m 'Shut down or reboot?' \
        -B 'Poweroff' 'systemctl poweroff' \
        -B 'Reboot' 'systemctl reboot'

    # Lock on demand: a session op, so Ctrl tier, l = lock. (Not $mod+l: that
    # is `focus right` ($right = l) -- the same collision that moved the exit
    # binding off $mod+Shift+l on 2025-11-10.)
    # Appearance is in the swaylock package, not inline -- see swaylock/config.
    bindsym --no-repeat $mod+Ctrl+l exec swaylock -f

    # Toggle Waybar. Sway's own bar hiding (`mode hide`) is dead here because
    # Waybar's `ipc` option is off, so it ignores the bar protocol and reads
    # its own config; the toggle goes through Waybar's SIGUSR1 handler
    # instead. See ### Status bar below for why Waybar is launched as sway's
    # bar in the first place.
    bindsym --no-repeat $mod+w exec killall -SIGUSR1 waybar

    # Notifications: dismiss the newest one on screen, a press each. The
    # keyboard's way to a critical one -- the battery at 10 percent -- which
    # otherwise waits for a click. Your attention, so the bare tier; n, as
    # GNOME's Super+n reaches its notifications (2026-09-30).
    bindsym --no-repeat $mod+n exec makoctl dismiss

#
# Launchers
#
    # $mod+d is the app menu: d for drun, the launchers' name for the
    # .desktop list, and upstream's launcher key besides. It sat on $mod+a --
    # GNOME's Super+A app grid --
    # until 2026-09-28, when a went to focus parent. $mod+f is files, opening
    # at ~/Desktop; the home-folder launcher went the same day. (GNOME's own
    # map on the same machines is per-machine dconf and parts from this file
    # on several keys; the record of 2026-09-30 lists them.)
    # Terminals answer to Return alone since 2026-09-29, so that Return becomes
    # the one habit, under GNOME as well: $mod+t and Shift+t went, and t is
    # free. WezTerm stays on Shift+Return.
    bindsym --no-repeat $mod+Return exec $term
    bindsym --no-repeat $mod+Shift+Return exec $term2
    bindsym --no-repeat $mod+b      exec brave-browser
    bindsym --no-repeat $mod+c      exec google-chrome-stable
    # A toggle since 2026-09-29: a second press closes the menu that the first
    # opened, where before each press opened another copy. pkill's exit status
    # is the test -- 0 when it found one to close, so the launch runs only when
    # none was open. -x matches the process name whole, never a command line.
    # rofi since 2026-09-30, wofi behind it on Shift (the third clause).
    bindsym --no-repeat $mod+d      exec pkill -x rofi || $menu
    bindsym --no-repeat $mod+Shift+d exec pkill -x wofi || $menu2
    bindsym --no-repeat $mod+f      exec nautilus --new-window ~/Desktop
    # adelotype, the typeface scratchpad, since 2026-09-29 on both machines;
    # under GNOME its tools/launcher.sh binds the same key. The plain editor
    # keeps the key with Shift: --new-window, as GNOME's key had it, because
    # Text Editor is single-instance and, launched bare while a window is
    # open, only re-presents that window, which sway does not act on -- the
    # key appeared to do nothing (measured on a nested sway, 2026-09-30).
    bindsym --no-repeat $mod+x      exec bash ~/Desktop/adelotype/tools/launcher.sh run
    bindsym --no-repeat $mod+Shift+x exec gnome-text-editor --new-window

#
# Windows
#
    # q closes the focused window, and only a window. Sway's bare `kill`
    # closes the focused container and all its children (sway(5)), so after
    # $mod+a on a workspace whose windows sit straight on it, every window
    # there would close -- and a is the key under q. Only a window carries a
    # pid in the tree, so that is the test. The jq program holds no string
    # literal on purpose: sway un-escapes a config line once before storing
    # it, so an escaped quote meant for the shell would arrive bare (measured
    # 2026-09-30). Not `[con_id=__focused__ ...] kill`: with a workspace
    # focused that criterion matches every window on every workspace
    # (measured).
    bindsym --no-repeat $mod+q exec sh -c 'swaymsg -t get_tree | jq -e "[.. | objects | select(.focused == true)] | first | .pid" >/dev/null && swaymsg -q kill'

    # Make the focused window fullscreen
    bindsym --no-repeat $mod+Shift+f fullscreen

    # Toggle the focused window between tiling and floating
    bindsym --no-repeat $mod+Shift+space floating toggle

    # Swap focus between the tiling area and the floating area
    bindsym --no-repeat $mod+space focus mode_toggle

    # Drag floating windows with $mod + left mouse button, resize with $mod +
    # right. Despite the name this also works on tiled windows. `inverse` swaps
    # the two buttons.
    floating_modifier $mod normal

    # A middle click on a fold's tab title closes that window, as on a browser
    # tab. Mouse bindings fire only over a title bar unless flagged (man 5
    # sway, bindsym), and only folds draw titles here, so a middle click
    # inside a window still pastes (measured on a nested sway, 2026-09-30).
    # A three-finger tap on a strip is a middle click too.
    bindsym button2 kill

    # A window asking to come forward -- a link opened into a running browser,
    # a notification clicked -- takes focus when it is already on screen and
    # is marked urgent otherwise (i3's default; sway's own is urgent alone,
    # which turned the focused workspace's chip orange while the window sat
    # in view; measured 2026-09-30).
    focus_on_window_activation smart

#
# Focus
#
    bindsym $mod+$left  focus left
    bindsym $mod+$down  focus down
    bindsym $mod+$up    focus up
    bindsym $mod+$right focus right

    bindsym $mod+Left   focus left
    bindsym $mod+Down   focus down
    bindsym $mod+Up     focus up
    bindsym $mod+Right  focus right

    # The tree: a climbs toward the root, z comes back down toward the windows
    # (z sits under a). a is upstream's, in both sway's and i3's defaults;
    # focus child has no upstream key. See the Layout section. Both stay on
    # the bare tier however rarely pressed: they move attention, as hjkl do
    # (settled 2026-09-29; DECISIONS.md, that date).
    bindsym $mod+a focus parent
    bindsym $mod+z focus child

#
# Moving windows
#
    # Each move first drops a pending $mod+Shift+v/b split (`split none`), so one press
    # always moves the window. Left to itself sway spends the first press on a
    # set window lifting it out of its invisible column, and moves nothing
    # unless a column is beside it (measured 2026-09-28). On a window that is
    # not set, `split none` fails harmlessly and the move is exactly as before
    # (measured in five cases). Not redundant: keep the prefix.
    bindsym $mod+Shift+$left  split none; move left
    bindsym $mod+Shift+$down  split none; move down
    bindsym $mod+Shift+$up    split none; move up
    bindsym $mod+Shift+$right split none; move right

    bindsym $mod+Shift+Left   split none; move left
    bindsym $mod+Shift+Down   split none; move down
    bindsym $mod+Shift+Up     split none; move up
    bindsym $mod+Shift+Right  split none; move right

#
# Workspaces
#
    # Walk workspaces on the current output. prev/next_on_output cycle only
    # among workspaces that actually exist, so they skip unopened numbers.
    bindsym $mod+$alt+$left  workspace prev_on_output
    bindsym $mod+$alt+$right workspace next_on_output
    bindsym $mod+$alt+Left   workspace prev_on_output
    bindsym $mod+$alt+Right  workspace next_on_output

    # Three-finger swipes walk this monitor's workspaces, as GNOME's do on the
    # same touchpad: the content follows the fingers, so a swipe left brings
    # in the next one. The same commands as the walk above, so they skip
    # unopened numbers and wrap at the ends (GNOME stops there). Two-finger
    # scrolling still reaches the app. Inert without a touchpad (2026-09-30).
    bindgesture swipe:3:left  workspace next_on_output
    bindgesture swipe:3:right workspace prev_on_output

    # Same, carrying the focused window along. `move container` does not follow
    # focus, hence the second command in each chain. Verified correct including
    # the case where the move empties (and so destroys) the current workspace.
    bindsym $mod+$alt+Shift+$left  move container to workspace prev_on_output; workspace prev_on_output
    bindsym $mod+$alt+Shift+$right move container to workspace next_on_output; workspace next_on_output
    bindsym $mod+$alt+Shift+Left   move container to workspace prev_on_output; workspace prev_on_output
    bindsym $mod+$alt+Shift+Right  move container to workspace next_on_output; workspace next_on_output

    # The other monitor, on the same modifier: up/down, because on bigfed the
    # MSI stands above the Samsung. +Shift carries the focused window -- or the
    # column selected with $mod+a -- and follows it, like the row walk above.
    # One press, where a directional move out of a row first restacks the
    # workspace (measured 2026-09-28). On a single screen, as on fedxps, these
    # do nothing (measured). The carries are --no-repeat: held, they would
    # bounce the window between the monitors at the repeat rate.
    bindsym $mod+$alt+$up    focus output up
    bindsym $mod+$alt+$down  focus output down
    bindsym $mod+$alt+Up     focus output up
    bindsym $mod+$alt+Down   focus output down
    bindsym --no-repeat $mod+$alt+Shift+$up    move container to output up; focus output up
    bindsym --no-repeat $mod+$alt+Shift+$down  move container to output down; focus output down
    bindsym --no-repeat $mod+$alt+Shift+Up     move container to output up; focus output up
    bindsym --no-repeat $mod+$alt+Shift+Down   move container to output down; focus output down

    # Bounce back to the previously focused workspace. The third motion the
    # $alt prev/next pair leaves missing: those two walk the row, this one
    # returns to wherever you just were regardless of distance.
    bindsym --no-repeat $mod+Tab workspace back_and_forth

    # Jump to a workspace by number. Workspaces can carry any name, not just
    # numbers; 1-10 is simply the default scheme.
    bindsym $mod+1 workspace number 1
    bindsym $mod+2 workspace number 2
    bindsym $mod+3 workspace number 3
    bindsym $mod+4 workspace number 4
    bindsym $mod+5 workspace number 5
    bindsym $mod+6 workspace number 6
    bindsym $mod+7 workspace number 7
    bindsym $mod+8 workspace number 8
    bindsym $mod+9 workspace number 9
    bindsym $mod+0 workspace number 10

    # Send the focused window to a workspace by number
    bindsym --no-repeat $mod+Shift+1 move container to workspace number 1
    bindsym --no-repeat $mod+Shift+2 move container to workspace number 2
    bindsym --no-repeat $mod+Shift+3 move container to workspace number 3
    bindsym --no-repeat $mod+Shift+4 move container to workspace number 4
    bindsym --no-repeat $mod+Shift+5 move container to workspace number 5
    bindsym --no-repeat $mod+Shift+6 move container to workspace number 6
    bindsym --no-repeat $mod+Shift+7 move container to workspace number 7
    bindsym --no-repeat $mod+Shift+8 move container to workspace number 8
    bindsym --no-repeat $mod+Shift+9 move container to workspace number 9
    bindsym --no-repeat $mod+Shift+0 move container to workspace number 10

#
# Layout -- the tree tier, revived for bigfed 2026-09-28
#
    # Sway's middle tier: the containers between window and workspace. It was
    # dropped 2026-08-22, when one or two windows per workspace on the laptop
    # left focus parent nothing to select but the workspace itself. bigfed's
    # 5120-px Samsung restores the premise: columns of windows are the natural
    # arrangement there, and a column is a thing to act on whole. With
    # one selected ($mod+a), a new window opens as a new column beside it, and
    # $mod+$alt+Shift+up/down carries the column to the other monitor intact
    # (both measured 2026-09-28).
    #
    # Keys are upstream's where upstream coheres with the grammar above: a for
    # focus parent, in both sway's and i3's defaults, and v and b for the
    # splits, sway's letters, one tier up. z, for
    # focus child, has no upstream key; it sits under a. The pair is bound in
    # the Focus section. r keeps one meaning across tiers, reshape: $mod+r
    # resizes the window, $mod+Ctrl+r rotates the container around it -- also
    # the repair when an orthogonal move has restacked a row into a column
    # (measured). Upstream's $mod+e for the toggle carries no mnemonic here.
    #
    # Deliberately unbound:
    #
    # * stacking, 2026-09-29: the column's fold, `layout stacking`, earns its
    #   place in a narrow, crowded container, where a strip would cut every
    #   title short. He never has more than three or four windows on a
    #   workspace, and only for a while, so a strip never does; return to it
    #   if that changes.
    #
    # The declined alternatives are recorded in DECISIONS.md, 2026-09-28 and
    # 2026-09-29.
    #
    # Folds, 2026-09-29 (DECISIONS.md, that date). tabbed and stacking
    # are the other two shapes a container can take: the row collapsed to a
    # strip of titles (T in the tree), the column collapsed to a pile of them
    # (S). One window shows; the rest wait behind their titles. The tabs are
    # ordinary windows, so every binding above works unchanged inside a
    # fold: h/l walk the tabs, wrapping at the ends when nothing lies beyond
    # and leaving otherwise, j/k step out, Shift+hjkl
    # reorder and carry out, a selects the whole fold, Shift+v splits inside a
    # tab. $mod+Shift+s folds the container around the focused window into
    # tabs and unfolds it again; if that chord proves to bite, the valve is
    # promotion to the bare tier, as q and r had, on measured frequency, and
    # s is free there (2026-09-29). $mod+Ctrl+r unfolds
    # a fold to the split it came from. On a window sitting directly on the
    # workspace, sway wraps every window there into the fold, and that wrapper
    # forgets the split it came from: a column unfolds as a row, and one
    # $mod+Ctrl+r restores it; once one window is left in the wrapper the bar
    # shows a stray beside until $mod+Shift+b (measured 2026-09-30). Where the
    # fold began with $mod+Shift+v on a window alone on its workspace, that
    # press leaves the workspace's own below showing, until $mod+Shift+v
    # (measured 2026-10-01). All measured
    # on a nested sway 1.11, 2026-09-29, where a bare
    # `layout toggle` proved the same as `toggle split`, whatever the man
    # page says of it.
    # $mod+Shift+v and $mod+Shift+b are toggles: sway-split (bin/) sets the
    # focused window so the next opens below or beside it, cancels the state
    # on a second press of the same key, flips it on the other, and wakes
    # Waybar's marker. On a window alone on its workspace, beside is the row
    # it already has, so no marker shows (2026-09-29; the single key of
    # 2026-09-28 was $mod+v). Called by path, because a sway session's PATH
    # lacks ~/bin: GDM starts sway without a login shell (measured
    # 2026-09-28). Suite: tests/sway-split/run.sh.
    bindsym --no-repeat $mod+Shift+v exec ~/bin/sway-split below
    bindsym --no-repeat $mod+Shift+b exec ~/bin/sway-split beside
    bindsym --no-repeat $mod+Shift+s layout toggle tabbed split
    bindsym --no-repeat $mod+Ctrl+r  layout toggle split

#
# Scratchpad
#
    # The scratchpad is a bag of holding for windows: send one away, get it back
    # later. With several inside, `scratchpad show` cycles through them.
    bindsym --no-repeat $mod+Shift+minus move scratchpad
    bindsym --no-repeat $mod+minus scratchpad show

#
# Hardware and media
#
    # --locked keeps these working while swaylock is up, so volume and
    # brightness stay adjustable on a locked machine. (Inside the resize mode
    # they are dead, as every binding outside the mode is.)
    # wpctl, PipeWire's own tool, because it takes a ceiling: -l 1.0 stops
    # the raise key at 100 percent, as GNOME's keys stop here
    # (allow-volume-above-100-percent is false) and as Waybar's scroll does
    # (max-volume, default 100); a level already above 100 percent comes down
    # to 100 on the next raise. pactl, upstream's choice, added 5 percent
    # with no ceiling, on into software gain. The steps are the same 5
    # percent, on the cubic scale the bar shows (2026-09-30).
    bindsym --locked --no-repeat XF86AudioMute exec wpctl set-mute \@DEFAULT_AUDIO_SINK@ toggle
    bindsym --locked XF86AudioLowerVolume exec wpctl set-volume \@DEFAULT_AUDIO_SINK@ 5%-
    bindsym --locked XF86AudioRaiseVolume exec wpctl set-volume -l 1.0 \@DEFAULT_AUDIO_SINK@ 5%+
    bindsym --locked --no-repeat XF86AudioMicMute exec wpctl set-mute \@DEFAULT_AUDIO_SOURCE@ toggle

    # Brightness-down reaches 0, on purpose: that switches the panel's backlight
    # off, which is how he blanks the laptop by hand, and one press up brings
    # it back (his word, 2026-09-30, when a 5 percent floor had been tried for
    # an evening). Nothing in it harms the panel. -c backlight pins the class,
    # so a machine with no backlight fails instead of dimming an LED; -q keeps
    # each press out of the journal.
    bindsym --locked XF86MonBrightnessDown exec brightnessctl -q -c backlight set 5%-
    bindsym --locked XF86MonBrightnessUp   exec brightnessctl -q -c backlight set 5%+

    # Switch every pointing device off, and back on -- the touchpad on
    # fedxps, the mouse on bigfed by whichever path it arrives -- and say
    # which way it went: otherwise the pointer simply stops, and m sits just
    # below j. ~/bin/sway-pointer (its header has the whole contract; suite
    # tests/sway-pointer) addresses each device by sway's identifier, never
    # by type: `input type:touchpad` reached nothing on bigfed, and `input
    # type:pointer` would take the keyboard, whose knob makes its node a
    # pointer to libinput as well (2026-10-03). A device asleep at the press
    # is remembered and wakes off; Waybar shows "pointer off" meanwhile. The
    # words are sway's own (send_events in `swaymsg -t get_inputs`). A reload
    # ($mod+Ctrl+c) turns everything back on. By path: a sway session's PATH
    # lacks ~/bin.
    bindsym --no-repeat $mod+m exec ~/bin/sway-pointer toggle

#
# Screenshots
#
    # Every capture ends in a notification saying where it went (low urgency:
    # 3 s, the muted border), with the image as its icon, as GNOME's do. Files
    # land in Pictures/Screenshots, GNOME's folder, so both sessions' captures
    # sit together, named in grim's own format; the name is made here because
    # grim chooses its default name without printing it. xdg-user-dir reads
    # ~/.config/user-dirs.dirs, the file grim reads for its default directory
    # (DECISIONS.md item 5). Two captures in the same second share a name, and
    # the second replaces the first. --no-repeat: held, Print took 25 shots a
    # second (2026-09-30).
    bindsym --no-repeat Print exec sh -c 'd=$(xdg-user-dir PICTURES)/Screenshots && mkdir -p "$d" && f=$d/$(date +%Y%m%d_%Hh%Mm%Ss)_grim.png && grim "$f" && notify-send -u low -i "$f" "Screenshot saved" "$f"'

    # Region -> clipboard, and region -> file. slurp prints the geometry, its
    # outline in the desktop's accent (no # before the hex: inside $(...) it
    # would start a shell comment); Esc, or a click without a drag, cancels
    # it: exit 1, nothing printed.
    #
    # The guard is not decoration. Written the obvious way as
    # `grim -g "$(slurp)" - | wl-copy`, cancelling the selection leaves an empty
    # geometry, grim fails, and wl-copy still runs with nothing on stdin -- which
    # silently WIPES the clipboard. Testing both the exit status and the string
    # means a cancelled screenshot changes nothing at all. For the same reason
    # the clipboard binding captures to a temporary file and copies only after
    # grim succeeds: through a pipe, any grim failure after a good selection
    # would wipe the clipboard the same way (measured 2026-09-30).
    bindsym --no-repeat Shift+Print exec sh -c 'g=$(slurp -c 0088FF) && [ -n "$g" ] && t=$(mktemp) && { grim -g "$g" "$t" && wl-copy -t image/png < "$t" && notify-send -u low "Screenshot copied" "$g"; rm -f "$t"; }'
    bindsym --no-repeat $mod+Print  exec sh -c 'g=$(slurp -c 0088FF) && [ -n "$g" ] && d=$(xdg-user-dir PICTURES)/Screenshots && mkdir -p "$d" && f=$d/$(date +%Y%m%d_%Hh%Mm%Ss)_grim.png && grim -g "$g" "$f" && notify-send -u low -i "$f" "Screenshot saved" "$f"'

    # The focused window alone, as GNOME's Alt+Print takes it: its content,
    # without the border (window_rect inside rect, sway-ipc(7)); with a
    # column or fold selected by $mod+a, the whole of it. grim -T, which would
    # capture a window by its own buffer, needs a protocol sway 1.11 does not
    # offer (measured 2026-09-30).
    bindsym --no-repeat $alt+Print exec sh -c 'set -- $(swaymsg -t get_tree | jq ".. | select(.focused?) | if .pid then (.rect.x + .window_rect.x, .rect.y + .window_rect.y, .window_rect.width, .window_rect.height) else (.rect.x, .rect.y, .rect.width, .rect.height) end") && [ "$#" = 4 ] && [ "$3" -gt 0 ] && d=$(xdg-user-dir PICTURES)/Screenshots && mkdir -p "$d" && f=$d/$(date +%Y%m%d_%Hh%Mm%Ss)_grim.png && grim -g "$1,$2 $3x$4" "$f" && notify-send -u low -i "$f" "Screenshot saved" "$f"'

#
# Resize mode
#
mode "resize" {
    # left/right shrink and grow width; up/down shrink and grow height
    bindsym $left  resize shrink width 10px
    bindsym $down  resize grow height 10px
    bindsym $up    resize shrink height 10px
    bindsym $right resize grow width 10px

    bindsym Left   resize shrink width 10px
    bindsym Down   resize grow height 10px
    bindsym Up     resize shrink height 10px
    bindsym Right  resize grow width 10px

    # Return to default mode -- and $mod+r again, so the chord that entered
    # leaves as well (i3's default config binds its entry key inside the mode;
    # sway's dropped it), a toggle like $mod+d. Waybar shows "resize" while
    # the mode holds the keys (waybar/config, sway/mode).
    bindsym Return mode "default"
    bindsym Escape mode "default"
    bindsym $mod+r mode "default"
}
bindsym --no-repeat $mod+r mode "resize"


### Status bar

# Waybar is launched as sway's bar rather than via `exec_always waybar`. The
# trade-off, deliberately taken: a reload replaces Waybar instead of stacking
# a second one (sway closes the old bar's connection and starts a fresh bar),
# and brings back a bar that has died -- $mod+Ctrl+c after a crash, or once a
# bad style.css is fixed -- but sway does not restart a bar that dies on its
# own: it stays gone until the next reload (sway 1.11 sway/config/bar.c:
# nothing restarts it; 2026-09-30). The command runs without a shell, as one
# word: an argument here ('waybar -l debug') is exec'd as a file of that
# name, and the session has no bar, silently. Sway's own bar settings
# (position, mode, hidden_state, colors, font) are dead config here --
# Waybar reads ~/.config/waybar/config and, with its `ipc` option off,
# ignores the bar protocol. So do not add settings to this block expecting
# them to take effect; change Waybar's own config. Sway appends `-b bar-0`
# to this command; Waybar ignores the id while `ipc` is off.
# Visibility is toggled with $mod+w -- see "Session, power, and the bar" above.
bar {
    swaybar_command waybar
}


### Appearance

# No titlebars on windows; a 2-px outline carries the focus signal. The one
# place a title is drawn is the strip of a fold (tabs, a stack), where the
# title is the tab itself. Two, deliberately (2026-09-30): the file said 3
# from the first commit, but a trailing comment on each line reached sway as
# extra words and default_border then kept its own 2 -- the width he had
# looked at all along, and judged right; the lines now say what is drawn.
# Tiling windows: border, no titlebar.
default_border pixel 2
# Floating windows too.
default_floating_border pixel 2

# Hide borders when a workspace holds only one tiled window.
smart_borders on

# New workspaces start as a row; a fold is made by hand ($mod+Shift+s), never by
# default.
workspace_layout default

# The strip of a fold is set in this font, and nothing else here is. Named, so
# the strip stops following fontconfig's monospace alias, which differs by
# machine (Bitstream Vera Sans Mono on fedxps, 2026-09-29); the terminals use
# the same face. Checked at runtime and rendered on a nested sway that day.
font pango:Source Code Pro 10

# Palette.
# $accent is the live one: dodger blue, the desktop's one accent since
# 2026-09-29 -- Waybar and wofi define the same value, each keeping a forest
# define beside it. It means where you are, or what is selected. $forest is
# the retired forest-green accent, kept so it can be swapped back by changing
# a single word below.
set $forest #228B22
set $accent #0088FF

# $urgent is shared with the rest of the desktop on purpose: Waybar's urgent
# workspace, mako's critical border, and swaylock's wrong-password ring all use
# this same colour, so "something needs you" looks identical everywhere. Needs
# you means waiting on you before you go on: an alert, or a state to leave --
# the resize mode, and since 2026-10-01 a pending split (the edge below, and
# Waybar's marker).
set $urgent #d08770

# Format: border  background  text  indicator  child_border
#
# The focused indicator is the split cue, in $urgent since 2026-10-01 (white
# from 2026-09-28): a pending split waits on you, as the resize mode does.
# Sway paints it on the edge where the next window will open, only while the
# focused window sits alone in its container -- the bottom edge after
# $mod+Shift+v, the right edge after $mod+Shift+b or $mod+Ctrl+r -- and it
# clears once a window lands there or the window is lifted out (measured
# 2026-09-28). Until 2026-09-28 it matched the border, so the state could not
# be seen. A lone window on a workspace has no border at all (smart_borders),
# so no cue there. An urgent window takes $urgent on its whole frame; the split
# cue is one edge of the focused window.
#
# background and text are drawn only in a fold's strip, since windows have no
# title bars. The focused title sits on the accent, the active title of an
# unfocused fold on grey, every other title white on black, so the active tab
# reads in both states (rendered on a nested sway, 2026-09-29).
client.focused          $accent  $accent #ffffff $urgent $accent
client.focused_inactive #5f676a  #5f676a #ffffff #5f676a #5f676a
client.unfocused        #434c5e  #000000 #ffffff #434c5e #434c5e
# urgent's background is the urgent colour too, so an urgent tab in a fold
# fills orange like the bar's urgent pill; on black it showed only a 1-px
# outline (measured 2026-09-30).
client.urgent           $urgent  $urgent #ffffff $urgent $urgent
# focused_tab_title colours the tab of a fold that holds a split while focus
# is inside that split; unset, it fell back to focused_inactive, grey, the
# look of a fold without focus (rendered on a nested sway, 2026-09-30). Three
# colours only: sway warns that indicator and child_border have no effect
# for this class.
client.focused_tab_title $accent  $accent #ffffff


### Deliberate omissions
#
# Recorded so they are not mistaken for oversights and "fixed" later:
#
# * No idle timers and no screen blanking, on either machine. The screen stays
#   on until dimmed by hand, because sway sessions here are long stretches of
#   focused work. This is the same posture as leaving Caffeine on under GNOME,
#   which he does on bigfed too (confirmed 2026-09-30: nothing turns off
#   unless he says so; his `screens-off` command is the on-demand form,
#   GNOME-only so far). Note that the swayidle line under "Includes and
#   startup" is NOT a timer -- it has no `timeout` clause at all, only
#   `before-sleep` and `lock`, so it can never blank or lock mid-work.
#
#   The hole this deliberately leaves closed as of 2026-08-13: logind's default
#   HandleLidSwitch=suspend applies, so closing the lid suspends, and before
#   2026-08-13 reopening it returned straight to an unlocked desktop (verified
#   by test). The before-sleep hook now locks first. With a second screen
#   attached logind ignores the lid instead (HandleLidSwitchDocked=ignore), so
#   a docked lid close neither suspends nor locks and the closed panel stays
#   an output; the bindswitch pair for that waits on a docked habit (record,
#   2026-09-30).


### Includes and startup

include /etc/sway/config.d/*

# This machine's own layer: hosts/<hostname>.conf beside this file, stowed
# with it (the hostname is fedxps or bigfed). What a monitor's serial or an
# input's identifier can key stays above, inert where that hardware is
# absent; a layer holds only what cannot be keyed that way -- a global, a
# startup exec, a chord one machine alone should have -- and adds, never
# rebinds. sway runs include paths through wordexp, so $(uname -n) is
# evaluated here, at every load and reload, after every setting above. A
# missing layer is silent (sway logs it only at debug level), so
# tests/desktop/run.sh checks that this machine's is linked, and validates
# each layer inlined, since `sway --validate` passes an error inside an
# included file. Relative on purpose: a test copy of this file elsewhere
# finds no layer beside it and loads none (2026-09-30).
include hosts/$(uname -n).conf

# The session file (sway/wayland-session.desktop, installed by hand as
# /usr/local/share/wayland-sessions/sway.desktop) starts sway from a login
# shell, so sway and everything it launches inherit the personal PATH. This
# hands the same PATH to the user manager and to D-Bus, for what starts as a
# service instead -- a DBusActivatable app opened from wofi (Nautilus, Loupe,
# Ghostty's entry) and the user units -- as gnome-session does under GNOME.
# Without the session file sway's PATH is the system's, and this changes
# nothing (TODO.md item 29).
exec dbus-update-activation-environment --systemd PATH

# Lock the screen before the machine suspends, and when logind is asked to
# lock the session (`loginctl lock-session`, or any Handle*=lock action) --
# and ONLY then. There is no `timeout` clause, so this never blanks or locks
# during work; see "Deliberate omissions" above, which also records the lid
# hole this closes.
#
# `-w` (swayidle) plus `-f` (swaylock) is the pairing the swayidle man page
# prescribes: -f detaches only once the screen is actually locked, and -w makes
# swayidle hold logind's inhibitor until then, so the machine cannot suspend
# with the desktop still visible. logind caps that wait at InhibitDelayMaxSec
# (5s here); swaylock comes up well inside it. Nothing restarts swayidle if
# it dies (it exits on its own if its D-Bus connection drops), and a shut lid
# then suspends unlocked: sway/CLAUDE.md holds the gauge and the restore line.
#
# `exec`, not `exec_always`: a reload must not spawn a second swayidle.
exec swayidle -w before-sleep 'swaylock -f' lock 'swaylock -f'

# The power button opens the machine nag (Session, power, and the bar)
# instead of powering the machine off. logind acts on the key itself --
# HandlePowerKey=poweroff, a clean shutdown on one press, no confirmation --
# unless some process holds its handle-power-key inhibitor.
# gnome-settings-daemon holds it under GNOME, where power-button-action is
# 'nothing' on both machines; under sway nothing did (measured 2026-09-28 on
# bigfed, 2026-09-30 on fedxps). The holder lives exactly as long as sway:
# swaymsg waits on sway's IPC and exits with it, on a clean exit and on a
# crash, releasing the inhibitor. Not `sleep infinity`: sway starts each exec
# in its own session and never reaps it, and logind here leaves a closed
# session's processes running, so that holder would outlive the session.
# logind honours the lock only while this session is the active one: at a
# console or the greeter the button powers off as before. `exec`, not
# `exec_always`: one holder per session.
exec systemd-inhibit --what=handle-power-key --mode=block --who=sway --why='power key: see sway/config' swaymsg -t subscribe '["shutdown"]'

# NetworkManager secret agent -- pulls 802.1x creds from gnome-keyring -- and
# the tray icon. Started here because nothing runs XDG autostart under sway:
# sway-systemd ships the snippet that would (95-xdg-desktop-autostart.conf)
# in /usr/share/sway-systemd, not /etc/sway/config.d, so
# sway-xdg-autostart.target never starts. Enabling it would start every
# entry systemd makes a unit for -- nm-applet a second time among them -- a
# separate decision (2026-09-30). `exec`, not `exec_always`, so a reload does
# not stack up applets. Not installed on bigfed: one harmless "command not
# found" line per login there.
exec nm-applet --indicator
